    {"kind": "h2", "text": "4.4  My Miners (09)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "My Miners is the profit-and-loss view per machine: monthly cost, estimated monthly revenue, and progress toward the target for every miner in the fleet. A machine whose estimated revenue sits below its cost for days is a decision waiting to be made: re-validate it, reprice it, move it, or kill it. This view exists so those decisions come from numbers, not vibes."},
    {"kind": "h2", "text": "4.5  Monitoring (10)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "Monitoring answers the alive question with operational telemetry: started miners, daily emission, monthly cost, uptime, and latency. It is deliberately distinct from the DevOps Engine, which answers the competitive question. When something looks wrong, Monitoring tells you whether the process is up and responding before you start blaming the strategy."},
    {"kind": "h2", "text": "4.6  Optimization (11)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "Optimization is the what-should-I-do-next view: switch GPU, switch subnet, or apply a runtime tune, each with expected impact and each executed through the same snapshot-then-push pipeline as the Validator Lab. Use it when the Validator Lab is satisfied but profits are not, and read its suggestions against the Opportunities ranking before committing hardware moves."},

    # ================= CHAPTER 5 =================
    {"kind": "h1", "text": "Chapter 5  ·  Platform and Administration (Views 12-15)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "The last group covers the platform itself: the aggregate numbers, the plumbing, the procedures, and the people. These views are visited less often than Operations but they are the first stop when something behaves strangely, and they are where access and accountability live."},
    {"kind": "h2", "text": "5.1  Analytics (12)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "Analytics aggregates platform-level summary numbers: spend, earnings history, and trends over time. It is the weekly review surface, the place to confirm that the aggregate direction matches the per-machine story told by My Miners. If the two disagree, trust the machine-level view and go find the deployment that is dragging the average."},
    {"kind": "h2", "text": "5.2  System and Errors (13)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "System and Errors runs live health checks for every dependency the platform has: the chain RPC, the price feed, the RunPod API, and the GPU offer pipeline, plus a client-side runtime error log with resolution steps. Make it the first stop in any incident, because an outage in a data source looks exactly like a miner problem from the outside, and ten minutes of triage here saves an hour of pointless miner restarts."},
    {"kind": "h2", "text": "5.3  Runbook (14)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "The Runbook documents every trigger kind the DevOps Engine can fire, what it means, and what to do about it. It is the manual for the automation. Keep it open in a second tab during the first weeks of operation; by the second month the vocabulary will be muscle memory and the Runbook becomes the reference you hand to the next operator."},
    {"kind": "h2", "text": "5.4  Access Control (15)"},
    {"kind": "body", "text": "Access Control manages the five-operator base: one administrator and four member roles spanning operators and analysts. Codes are stored twice, once as a one-way scrypt hash used for verification and once encrypted so the admin panel can display the current codes without any chat transcription. Failed and successful sign-ins are audited, and the login route throttles repeated failures per address. Regenerate any code that has ever been pasted into a chat, and deactivate rather than delete accounts that leave the team."},

